{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QuantumObservableUniverse.DqTwist","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QuantumObservableUniverse.DqTwist","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.QuantumObservableUniverse.Instanton","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":11,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.dq_sq_eq_zero_of_q_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.dq_sq_eq_zero_of_q_one","kind":"theorem","line":257,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L257-L268","doc":"`q = 1` kills the curvature through the **scalar** `(1 − q⁻¹)`, leaving the\nprojector commutator untouched. `dq_classical` recovered from the formula. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.dq_sq_eq_zero_of_projComm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.dq_sq_eq_zero_of_projComm","kind":"theorem","line":245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L245-L255","doc":"**The sharp criterion**: a commuting vertical projector kills the curvature\nfor **every** `q`, classical or not.\n\nCompare `dq_sq_eq_zero_of_classical_via_twist`, which needs `q = 1`. The\ndeformation parameter was never the obstruction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.dq_sq_eq_curvature","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.dq_sq_eq_curvature","kind":"theorem","line":217,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L217-L243","doc":"**The curvature of `d_q`, exactly.**\n\nAssuming only `d² = 0`:\n\n    d_q ≫ d_q = (1 − q⁻¹) • (T_q ≫ κ ≫ d)\n\nThis is strictly stronger than every vanishing criterion above, which it\nimplies. Two readings matter:\n\n* the obstruction is **entirely** the projector commutator, scaled — there\n  is no other source of curvature;\n* the scaling is **first order** in `(1 − q⁻¹)`. `d_q` is a degree-zero\n  differential whose failure to square to zero lives in filtration degree\n  ≥ 1, which is precisely the condition under which filtering makes the\n  associated graded a genuine complex and pushes the curvature into a\n  higher differential. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.twist_comm_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.twist_comm_iff","kind":"theorem","line":201,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L201-L215","doc":"**The twist commutes exactly when the curvature vanishes.**\n\nSo `dq_sq_eq_zero_of_twist_comm`'s hypothesis was never about the twist as\nsuch: it is `(1 − q⁻¹) • κ = 0`, which `q = 1` achieves through the scalar\nand `κ = 0` achieves through the projector. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.d_comp_twist","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.d_comp_twist","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L188-L199","doc":"**Moving `d` past the twist costs `(1 − q⁻¹) • κ`.**\n\nThe identity `𝟙` half of the twist commutes with everything, so the entire\ndefect comes from the projector, scaled by the deformation coefficient. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.projComm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.projComm","kind":"def","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L178-L186","doc":"**The projector commutator** `κ = d ≫ π_V − π_V ≫ d`.\n\n`Instanton.lean`'s axiom justification names this cross-term in prose —\n*\"the vertical projection `π_V` does not commute with the classical\ndifferential `d`\"* — and its `hcross` hypothesis is about it. Here it is\nthe object rather than the sentence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.dq_sq_eq_zero_of_classical_via_twist","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.dq_sq_eq_zero_of_classical_via_twist","kind":"theorem","line":160,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L160-L171","doc":"**The classical case, derived from commutation rather than by unfolding.**\n\n`q = 1` makes the twist the identity, which commutes with everything — so\n`dq_classical`'s conclusion follows from the general theorem above. The\npoint is that `q = 1` is *sufficient because it trivialises the twist*, not\nbecause the deformation parameter is special. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.dq_sq_eq_zero_of_twist_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.dq_sq_eq_zero_of_twist_comm","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L136-L158","doc":"**The theorem this file exists for.**\n\nIf the twist commutes with the differential and `d² = 0`, then `d_q² = 0` —\nwith **no hypothesis on `q`**.\n\n`d_q ≫ d_q = T ≫ d ≫ T ≫ d = T ≫ T ≫ d ≫ d = 0`.\n\nSo the obstruction to `d_q` being a differential is **non-commutation of the\ntwist**, not non-classicality of the deformation parameter. This strictly\ngeneralises `CategoricalQExtDeriv.dq_classical`, which gets the conclusion\nonly at `q = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.twist_classical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.twist_classical","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L125-L134","doc":"At `q = 1` the twist is the identity — which is why `dq_classical` holds. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.dq_eq_twist_comp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.dq_eq_twist_comp","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L116-L123","doc":"**`d_q` is `d` twisted**: `d_q = T_q ≫ d`.\n\nComposition is bilinear, so the definition in `Instanton.lean` factors. This\nis what makes \"twist\" a literal description rather than an analogy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.DqTwist.twist","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.DqTwist.twist","kind":"def","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverse/DqTwist.lean#L107-L114","doc":"**The twist**: `T_q = qinv • 𝟙 + (1 − qinv) • π_V`.\n\nA deformation of the identity by the vertical projector, interpolating\nbetween `𝟙` at `qinv = 1` and `π_V` at `qinv = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}